WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:02.770
What would you do if you were given absolute
power?

00:00:02.770 --> 00:00:04.830
Would you use it to fix the world’s ills?

00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:08.360
Or would you use it to satisfy your own darkest
desires?

00:00:08.360 --> 00:00:13.600
It’s a question we’ve seen answered again
and again in history, but never so conclusively

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:18.630
as in the reign of Rome’s third emperor,
Gaius Julius Caesar Germanicus.

00:00:18.630 --> 00:00:25.279
Or, as you know him: Caligula.

00:00:25.279 --> 00:00:32.810
Born into a powerful military family, Caligula
rose up to become the most infamous emperor

00:00:32.810 --> 00:00:34.200
in Roman history.

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:38.860
During his four year reign, he indulged in
so much excess that 2,000 years later his

00:00:38.860 --> 00:00:43.340
name is still a byword for cruelty and perversion.

00:00:43.340 --> 00:00:48.120
You probably know the tales about Caligula
seducing his sister and making his horse a

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:49.120
consul.

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:53.320
You may not know that the rest of his life
was, if anything, even wilder.

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:59.150
Today, we take you inside the twisted world
of Rome’s maddest emperor.

00:00:59.150 --> 00:01:06.510
A Game of (Imperial) Thrones
When Gaius Julius Caesar Germanicus was born,

00:01:06.510 --> 00:01:11.920
on August 31, 12 AD, it was into a world of
privilege that would’ve made the average

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:12.979
Roman’s head spin.

00:01:12.979 --> 00:01:17.049
Gaius’s father was the general Germanicus,
a military hero of the empire.

00:01:17.049 --> 00:01:20.670
His mother, Agrippina the elder, came from
a family that had once been next in line to

00:01:20.670 --> 00:01:21.670
the imperial throne.

00:01:21.670 --> 00:01:24.790
To call the family elite would be to undersell
it.

00:01:24.790 --> 00:01:29.039
They were like if the Kennedys, Rothschilds,
Rockefellers, and the Queen of England had

00:01:29.039 --> 00:01:32.259
all joined into a single family unit.

00:01:32.259 --> 00:01:37.319
Still, Germanicus was a working general, and
for little Gaius that meant a childhood growing

00:01:37.319 --> 00:01:41.469
up in military camps on the Western frontier
along the Rhine.

00:01:41.469 --> 00:01:45.380
While stationed in these camps, Gaius was
given a little soldier’s uniform to wear.

00:01:45.380 --> 00:01:49.399
The sight of this toddler parading around
tickled the soldiers serving under Germanicus

00:01:49.399 --> 00:01:52.520
so much that they gave him the nickname “Little
Boot”.

00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:55.719
Or, to render it in Latin: Caligula.

00:01:55.719 --> 00:02:00.079
But while life may have seemed sweet for little
Caligula on the frontier, back in Rome, an

00:02:00.079 --> 00:02:01.689
almighty storm was brewing.

00:02:01.689 --> 00:02:06.680
For nearly four decades, the imperial throne
had been in the hands of Augustus.

00:02:06.680 --> 00:02:11.480
But with the First Citizen now nearing the
end of his life, dark political forces were

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:12.480
stirring.

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:18.439
Augustus’s plan had been for one of Agrippina’s
two brothers to inherit his throne, but both

00:02:18.439 --> 00:02:19.700
had died young.

00:02:19.700 --> 00:02:24.159
After falling out with a third successor,
Augustus was forced to turn to a sullen general

00:02:24.159 --> 00:02:25.700
called Tiberius.

00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:30.340
Tiberius was technically a hero of the empire,
but he was also something of a colossal dick.

00:02:30.340 --> 00:02:33.010
Nobody liked him, not even Augustus.

00:02:33.010 --> 00:02:37.380
The only way the dying emperor could get Tiberius
on the throne without a riot was to force

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:41.500
Tiberius to adopt Caligula’s dad, the beloved
Germanicus.

00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:47.519
That way, everyone could sleep soundly, knowing
that however terrible Tiberius was, Germanicus

00:02:47.519 --> 00:02:48.800
would soon be in charge.

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:52.470
You know that phrase ‘the best laid plans
of mice and men’?

00:02:52.470 --> 00:02:57.000
Well, Tiberius was about to do to Augustus’s
plans exactly what Jerry does to Tom with

00:02:57.000 --> 00:02:58.560
a stick of dynamite.

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:03.950
On August 19, 14 AD, Augustus finally shuffled
off this mortal coil.

00:03:03.950 --> 00:03:08.569
No sooner was he gone than Tiberius began
moving against Germanicus.

00:03:08.569 --> 00:03:12.659
Caligula’s family was taken from the Rhine
and transplanted to Syria.

00:03:12.659 --> 00:03:18.120
There, on October 10, 19 AD, Germanicus died
in mysterious circumstances.

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:22.000
Convinced Tiberius had poisoned her husband,
Agrippina agitated for justice.

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:25.760
It was at this point that Tiberius brought
the other boot crashing down.

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:31.019
Tiberius forbid Agrippina to remarry, and
exiled her to a remote island.

00:03:31.019 --> 00:03:33.069
Her eldest two sons were imprisoned.

00:03:33.069 --> 00:03:34.880
One of them committed suicide.

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:36.640
The other starved to death.

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:38.769
For Caligula, it was a life-changing upheaval.

00:03:38.769 --> 00:03:43.650
Although his grandmother shielded him from
Tiberius’s wrath, he was suddenly left without

00:03:43.650 --> 00:03:46.140
parents, without brothers, without hope.

00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:49.709
His only consolation may have been that his
three sisters were also allowed to live.

00:03:49.709 --> 00:03:52.120
Certainly, the experience made them close.

00:03:52.120 --> 00:03:56.930
So close, that it was often rumored Caligula
was sleeping with the youngest, Julia Drusilla.

00:03:56.930 --> 00:03:59.090
But Tiberius wasn’t just cunning.

00:03:59.090 --> 00:04:00.239
He was also cruel.

00:04:00.239 --> 00:04:05.719
In AD 31 or 32, the nineteen year old Caligula
was suddenly summoned to Tiberius’s villa

00:04:05.719 --> 00:04:07.400
on the island of Capri.

00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:11.920
From now on, the emperor informed him, he
would have to live alongside the man who’d

00:04:11.920 --> 00:04:13.150
destroyed his family.

00:04:13.150 --> 00:04:15.900
It was a bizarre, perverted thing for Tiberius
to do.

00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:19.489
It would also be a catastrophe for the entire
empire.

00:04:19.489 --> 00:04:26.990
Making a Monster
So, here’s a couple of things you should

00:04:26.990 --> 00:04:29.010
know about Tiberius.

00:04:29.010 --> 00:04:33.090
The first is that Tiberius was basically Roman
Stalin.

00:04:33.090 --> 00:04:37.530
He ruled through fear, instigating a wave
of treason trials in which people were convicted

00:04:37.530 --> 00:04:41.960
by kangaroo courts, executed, and their bodies
dumped in the River Tiber.

00:04:41.960 --> 00:04:46.370
The second is that he did all this while staying
away from Rome itself.

00:04:46.370 --> 00:04:50.949
In AD 26, the old despot retreated to a villa
on Capri where he had a good time while a

00:04:50.949 --> 00:04:53.759
flunky did all the actual repressing.

00:04:53.759 --> 00:04:57.500
Incidentally, a “good time” for Tiberius
generally meant everyone else having an extremely

00:04:57.500 --> 00:04:58.639
bad time.

00:04:58.639 --> 00:05:04.050
If we believe the surviving sources, Tiberius’s
time on Capri was passed satisfying himself

00:05:04.050 --> 00:05:08.419
with underage boys and girls and then having
them hurled off the cliffs to their deaths.

00:05:08.419 --> 00:05:14.430
Oh, and he also liked to have the odd person
publicly tortured because… well, why not?

00:05:14.430 --> 00:05:16.780
It was into this world that Caligula was summoned.

00:05:16.780 --> 00:05:20.200
It’s impossible to say why Tiberius called
Caligula to him.

00:05:20.200 --> 00:05:21.699
Maybe it was a sick game.

00:05:21.699 --> 00:05:25.860
Tiberius’s flunkies would often question
the boy about his dead family, goading him

00:05:25.860 --> 00:05:26.860
to lash out.

00:05:26.860 --> 00:05:28.590
But Caligula wasn’t stupid.

00:05:28.590 --> 00:05:33.610
He kept his mouth shut, and deferred to Tiberius
more like a servant than an adopted family

00:05:33.610 --> 00:05:34.610
member.

00:05:34.610 --> 00:05:36.330
So Tiberius never got around to killing the
boy.

00:05:36.330 --> 00:05:39.880
Not only that, he seems to have grown to like
him.

00:05:39.880 --> 00:05:43.760
Before long, Tiberius was allowing Caligula
to indulge in orgies of his own.

00:05:43.760 --> 00:05:47.470
To arrange and watch sadistic tortures at
his leisure.

00:05:47.470 --> 00:05:51.729
It’s said that, after seeing how much the
quiet Caligula enjoyed watching others suffer,

00:05:51.729 --> 00:05:55.530
Tiberius remarked:
"I am nursing a viper for the Roman people”

00:05:55.530 --> 00:05:58.590
If he really believed that, though, Tiberius
didn’t show it.

00:05:58.590 --> 00:06:04.490
At some point in the mid-AD 30s, Tiberius
named Caligula and Caligula’s cousin Gemellus

00:06:04.490 --> 00:06:05.740
joint heirs to the empire.

00:06:05.740 --> 00:06:10.039
Maybe if Tiberius knew what the quiet young
man had been up to on Capri, he would have

00:06:10.039 --> 00:06:11.520
been more cautious.

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:15.800
Caligula later claimed he liked to get up
in the dead of night and creep into Tiberius’s

00:06:15.800 --> 00:06:20.259
bedroom, where he would hold a dagger over
the old man, ready to plunge it in.

00:06:20.259 --> 00:06:24.770
Only the voice of the dead Augustus stopped
him from doing the deed.

00:06:24.770 --> 00:06:28.870
Less Looney Tunes, Caligula also worked behind
the scenes to forge an alliance with the powerful

00:06:28.870 --> 00:06:33.600
Pretorian Guard prefect Naevius Sutorius Macro.

00:06:33.600 --> 00:06:36.270
When the time came, Caligula wanted the guard
on his side.

00:06:36.270 --> 00:06:39.229
The time came On March 16, 37 AD.

00:06:39.229 --> 00:06:42.340
That morning, word went out that Tiberius
had died in his sleep.

00:06:42.340 --> 00:06:44.680
Rome’s tyrant emperor was dead!

00:06:44.680 --> 00:06:48.590
There are a few theories about how Tiberius
died, and one prominent one is that Caligula

00:06:48.590 --> 00:06:50.520
suffocated the old man with a pillow.

00:06:50.520 --> 00:06:55.699
But even if this is just later writers adding
narrative spice to the tale, the result was

00:06:55.699 --> 00:06:56.699
the same.

00:06:56.699 --> 00:06:58.659
In Rome, the people went nuts.

00:06:58.659 --> 00:07:02.650
They demanded Tiberius’s body be dragged
through the streets and hurled into the river,

00:07:02.650 --> 00:07:06.780
as all those executed at his treason trials
had been.

00:07:06.780 --> 00:07:11.159
While Rome was busy partying, out on Capri,
Caligula was putting his plan into motion.

00:07:11.159 --> 00:07:14.240
With Tiberius gone, the Praetorian Guard came
out for Caligula.

00:07:14.240 --> 00:07:16.260
Gemellus was placed under house arrest.

00:07:16.260 --> 00:07:18.319
He’d never taste freedom again.

00:07:18.319 --> 00:07:22.400
His rival imprisoned, Caligula rode to Rome
and presented himself to the people as their

00:07:22.400 --> 00:07:23.400
new emperor.

00:07:23.400 --> 00:07:25.470
This… actually wasn’t as hard as you might
think.

00:07:25.470 --> 00:07:27.030
People had hated Tiberius.

00:07:27.030 --> 00:07:31.169
The whole city was seriously pumped he was
gone, and now they were being told that his

00:07:31.169 --> 00:07:36.550
replacement would be the son of their beloved
general Germanicus?

00:07:36.550 --> 00:07:41.750
To paraphrase Philip J. Fry, Rome was basically
like “just shut up and take our throne!”

00:07:41.750 --> 00:07:46.069
Aged only 24, Caligula was elevated to undisputed
ruler of the empire.

00:07:46.069 --> 00:07:47.800
The people were ecstatic.

00:07:47.800 --> 00:07:53.160
As he rode into the city, they lined up and
screamed like he was Roman Elvis or something.

00:07:53.160 --> 00:07:58.569
It wouldn’t be long before Caligula was
making those same citizens scream for very

00:07:58.569 --> 00:08:00.590
different reasons.

00:08:00.590 --> 00:08:07.310
Bread and Circuses
So… this is it, right?

00:08:07.310 --> 00:08:11.289
The part where we finally get to all the juicy
stories about Caligula promoting his horse

00:08:11.289 --> 00:08:13.039
and marrying his sister?

00:08:13.039 --> 00:08:14.039
Nope!

00:08:14.039 --> 00:08:16.889
That’s all still down the road.

00:08:16.889 --> 00:08:20.169
Because here’s the thing about Caligula,
the mad emperor.

00:08:20.169 --> 00:08:24.479
When he first seized power, he was seen as
Caligula, the emperor everyone just couldn’t

00:08:24.479 --> 00:08:25.629
get enough of.

00:08:25.629 --> 00:08:26.629
Seriously.

00:08:26.629 --> 00:08:29.289
Caligula 1.0 was everything Rome had ever
dreamed of.

00:08:29.289 --> 00:08:31.750
He ended Tiberius’s treason trials.

00:08:31.750 --> 00:08:33.240
Restored confiscated property.

00:08:33.240 --> 00:08:34.659
Eased up on persecution.

00:08:34.659 --> 00:08:36.169
Lowered taxes.

00:08:36.169 --> 00:08:38.010
Gave the army a pay rise.

00:08:38.010 --> 00:08:43.390
As if this wasn’t enough, Caligula threw
endless gladiator games and feasts to celebrate

00:08:43.390 --> 00:08:44.990
his rise to power.

00:08:44.990 --> 00:08:46.250
Imagine you’re an ordinary Roman.

00:08:46.250 --> 00:08:49.000
For over twenty years, you’ve been living
in a state of fear under a tyrant who could

00:08:49.000 --> 00:08:50.330
have you killed at any time.

00:08:50.330 --> 00:08:53.800
And now there’s this new guy in town and
he’s giving you bread and circuses and a

00:08:53.800 --> 00:08:55.760
promise that he’s not gonna randomly kill
you?

00:08:55.760 --> 00:08:57.450
Clearly a massive step up.

00:08:57.450 --> 00:09:01.600
As 37 AD rolled on, people dared to hope that
maybe this was it.

00:09:01.600 --> 00:09:06.460
Maybe Tiberius had been a one-off, and all
the emperors were gonna be cool from now on.

00:09:06.460 --> 00:09:07.460
Pfft.

00:09:07.460 --> 00:09:08.920
Yeah, right.

00:09:08.920 --> 00:09:11.210
That October, Caligula suddenly took sick.

00:09:11.210 --> 00:09:12.779
It’s possible he was poisoned.

00:09:12.779 --> 00:09:18.399
At any rate, he spent the best part of a month
floating on the threshold between life and

00:09:18.399 --> 00:09:19.399
death.

00:09:19.399 --> 00:09:20.399
The people of Rome were appalled.

00:09:20.399 --> 00:09:22.680
They couldn’t lose their shiny new emperor!

00:09:22.680 --> 00:09:23.910
There was weeping in the streets.

00:09:23.910 --> 00:09:29.120
In the Senate, senators took turns to stand
up and beseech the Gods to spare Caligula.

00:09:29.120 --> 00:09:33.420
A handful even begged Jupiter to take them
in his place.

00:09:33.420 --> 00:09:35.140
Miraculously, these prayers worked.

00:09:35.140 --> 00:09:39.360
Caligula recovered, dragging his weakened
body back to the land of the living.

00:09:39.360 --> 00:09:43.510
If this was a horror movie, now would be the
point that we said, “However, it seemed

00:09:43.510 --> 00:09:46.589
he brought some evil spirit back with him…”

00:09:46.589 --> 00:09:50.880
Since this is history, though, we’ll simply
say Caligula came back changed.

00:09:50.880 --> 00:09:53.070
The popular emperor was gone.

00:09:53.070 --> 00:09:55.610
In his place was something much darker.

00:09:55.610 --> 00:10:00.850
Not long after recovering, Caligula invited
the senators who’d prayed for his life to

00:10:00.850 --> 00:10:01.850
visit him.

00:10:01.850 --> 00:10:04.860
There, he thanked them for asking Jupiter
to take them in his place.

00:10:04.860 --> 00:10:07.990
Then he asked when they were going to do it.

00:10:07.990 --> 00:10:12.230
No records exist of the conversation that
followed, but it likely went something like

00:10:12.230 --> 00:10:14.070
this:
“Your highness?”

00:10:14.070 --> 00:10:15.560
Asked the Senators, puzzled.

00:10:15.560 --> 00:10:16.720
“You know.

00:10:16.720 --> 00:10:17.720
It.”

00:10:17.720 --> 00:10:18.720
Replied Caligula.

00:10:18.720 --> 00:10:21.790
“You asked the Gods to let you die so I
may live.

00:10:21.790 --> 00:10:23.610
Well, I am alive.

00:10:23.610 --> 00:10:24.970
So I repeat.

00:10:24.970 --> 00:10:27.670
When are you going to die?”

00:10:27.670 --> 00:10:29.300
The senators had no choice.

00:10:29.300 --> 00:10:31.970
One by one, they were all forced to commit
suicide.

00:10:31.970 --> 00:10:37.019
It was the first sign of the madness that
was about to consume the empire.

00:10:37.019 --> 00:10:39.680
Now, we need to be careful here.

00:10:39.680 --> 00:10:44.510
Plenty of ancient and contemporary writers
like to use Caligula’s sickness as a transformative

00:10:44.510 --> 00:10:48.510
event, like he was a saint before and a devil
after.

00:10:48.510 --> 00:10:52.910
But we’ve already seen Caligula getting
his rocks off torturing people in Tiberius’s

00:10:52.910 --> 00:10:53.910
villa.

00:10:53.910 --> 00:10:59.320
We’ve seen him quietly dispose of his rival
to the throne and scheme his way to power.

00:10:59.320 --> 00:11:02.300
So, yes, Caligula became a monster after his
illness.

00:11:02.300 --> 00:11:05.180
But it’s not like the signs weren’t already
there.

00:11:05.180 --> 00:11:07.870
With the senators dead, Caligula returned
to public life.

00:11:07.870 --> 00:11:10.779
From the get-go it was clear something was
wrong.

00:11:10.779 --> 00:11:15.410
He ditched the robes and started dressing
in outlandish costumes, sometimes including

00:11:15.410 --> 00:11:16.740
in women’s clothes.

00:11:16.740 --> 00:11:19.410
He claimed he was plagued by endless headaches.

00:11:19.410 --> 00:11:23.839
You can almost imagine the people of Rome,
giving one another sidelong glances and wondering

00:11:23.839 --> 00:11:25.780
what happened to their fun emperor.

00:11:25.780 --> 00:11:28.170
Well, Caligula still wanted to have fun.

00:11:28.170 --> 00:11:32.720
And Rome was about to discover that Caligula’s
definition of “fun” was even more terrifying

00:11:32.720 --> 00:11:34.220
than Tiberius’s.

00:11:34.220 --> 00:11:41.520
“I only have to give the word…”

00:11:41.520 --> 00:11:43.920
Before we go any further, there’s something
you need to understand about this period in

00:11:43.920 --> 00:11:44.920
Roman history.

00:11:44.920 --> 00:11:49.230
So many sources have been lost over the years
that we have barely any contemporary accounts

00:11:49.230 --> 00:11:50.230
of Caligula.

00:11:50.230 --> 00:11:54.529
The information we have on his life generally
comes from Suetonius’s book on the emperors,

00:11:54.529 --> 00:11:56.899
and Cassius Dio’s biography of Caligula.

00:11:56.899 --> 00:12:02.191
Both aren’t exactly great historians, and
both lived long after Caligula had died, with

00:12:02.191 --> 00:12:07.449
Dio not even being born until 120-odd years
after his death.

00:12:07.449 --> 00:12:11.440
To put it another way, expecting either of
these guys to give a pinpoint accurate account

00:12:11.440 --> 00:12:15.450
of Caligula’s reign is like expecting someone
born this very year to give an eyewitness

00:12:15.450 --> 00:12:18.070
account of the life of Otto von Bismarck.

00:12:18.070 --> 00:12:23.120
That being said, there’s likely a kernel
of truth in their otherwise suspect accounts,

00:12:23.120 --> 00:12:29.190
and even a kernel would be enough to rank
Caligula as one of the maddest rulers in history.

00:12:29.190 --> 00:12:33.230
It’s said that post-illness Caligula liked
to humiliate the Senate.

00:12:33.230 --> 00:12:36.880
Senators would be made to run along beside
his carriage for miles at a time through the

00:12:36.880 --> 00:12:37.880
streets of Rome.

00:12:37.880 --> 00:12:40.980
Those that refused would be humiliated in
other ways.

00:12:40.980 --> 00:12:43.980
Caligula would throw dinner parties for the
senators and their wives.

00:12:43.980 --> 00:12:48.110
Mid-way through the meal, he’d pick a wife
he liked the look of, take her into a backroom

00:12:48.110 --> 00:12:49.610
and force himself on her.

00:12:49.610 --> 00:12:53.959
He’d then return her to her husband, who’d
be forced to act like nothing had happened.

00:12:53.959 --> 00:12:58.760
Speaking of women, Caligula’s sexual appetites
were both enormous, and enormously cruel.

00:12:58.760 --> 00:13:03.390
In AD 37 or 38, for example, he went to the
wedding of Gaius Calpurnius Piso and Livia

00:13:03.390 --> 00:13:04.390
Orestilla.

00:13:04.390 --> 00:13:08.430
The emperor was so taken with Livia that he
ordered her to marry him instead.

00:13:08.430 --> 00:13:11.540
She did, he had his way with her, then divorced
her the next morning.

00:13:11.540 --> 00:13:15.000
As a final kicker, he forbade her to ever
marry Gaius.

00:13:15.000 --> 00:13:17.060
Livia wasn’t the only one.

00:13:17.060 --> 00:13:20.110
Lollia Paulina was also forced to divorce
her husband and marry Caligula, only to be

00:13:20.110 --> 00:13:22.480
ditched after barely six months.

00:13:22.480 --> 00:13:26.019
Then there are the other rumors, that Caligula
slept with everyone from whores, to young

00:13:26.019 --> 00:13:27.930
boys, to the male actor Mnester.

00:13:27.930 --> 00:13:32.300
But the most famous affair Caligula had was,
of course, with his sister.

00:13:32.300 --> 00:13:34.320
Remember Julia Drusilla?

00:13:34.320 --> 00:13:37.149
Back near the start of this video, we mentioned
it was rumored Caligula was sleeping with

00:13:37.149 --> 00:13:38.149
her.

00:13:38.149 --> 00:13:41.610
Well, once he became emperor, Caligula seems
to have gone out of his way to confirm those

00:13:41.610 --> 00:13:42.610
rumors.

00:13:42.610 --> 00:13:45.649
Drusilla was elevated to the status of emperor’s
wife.

00:13:45.649 --> 00:13:48.340
The army was required to take a loyalty oath
to her.

00:13:48.340 --> 00:13:49.820
The two were never apart.

00:13:49.820 --> 00:13:55.100
When Drusilla died in June, 38 AD, the heartbroken
Caligula had her declared a Goddess and forced

00:13:55.100 --> 00:13:56.430
people to worship her.

00:13:56.430 --> 00:14:01.029
Even with this weird relationship, though,
much of what you may have heard is untrue.

00:14:01.029 --> 00:14:05.430
That shocking scene in I, Claudius where Caligula
eats the fetus torn from Drusilla’s body?

00:14:05.430 --> 00:14:08.329
We’re extremely pleased to tell you that
was fiction.

00:14:08.329 --> 00:14:13.200
Still, Caligula’s cruelty and his insanity
were becoming hard to ignore.

00:14:13.200 --> 00:14:17.860
Cassius Dio has it that the emperor practiced
pulling terrifying facial expressions purely

00:14:17.860 --> 00:14:19.990
to put the fear of God into his subjects.

00:14:19.990 --> 00:14:23.019
At one party, he allegedly burst out laughing.

00:14:23.019 --> 00:14:25.639
When guests asked him what was funny, Caligula
replied:

00:14:25.639 --> 00:14:29.190
“I’ve just thought that I’ve only to
give the word and you’ll all have your throats

00:14:29.190 --> 00:14:30.550
cut.”

00:14:30.550 --> 00:14:32.120
It was more than just an idle threat.

00:14:32.120 --> 00:14:34.540
In AD 38, Caligula finally ordered Gemellus
executed.

00:14:34.540 --> 00:14:40.389
Incredibly, he had Macro, the Praetorian Guard
prefect who’d helped him seize power, executed

00:14:40.389 --> 00:14:41.440
shortly after.

00:14:41.440 --> 00:14:43.790
It was Caligula’s first major taste of blood.

00:14:43.790 --> 00:14:49.149
Evidently, he liked it enough to go back for
more.

00:14:49.149 --> 00:14:54.410
The Reign of Perversion
Throughout all this, the fun, pre-illness

00:14:54.410 --> 00:14:55.820
emperor hadn’t completely vanished.

00:14:55.820 --> 00:14:59.220
For all his bad rep, Caligula did do some
good stuff.

00:14:59.220 --> 00:15:03.300
He extended Rome’s aqueduct network, bringing
clean drinking water to many parts of the

00:15:03.300 --> 00:15:04.300
city.

00:15:04.300 --> 00:15:06.670
He had new ports constructed, opening the
empire up to more trade.

00:15:06.670 --> 00:15:10.709
There’s also reason to believe some of the
more infamous stories about him are false.

00:15:10.709 --> 00:15:12.870
That whole thing about making his horse a
consul?

00:15:12.870 --> 00:15:17.389
It’s thought Caligula was probably insulting
a senator’s intelligence and made a comment

00:15:17.389 --> 00:15:22.220
to the effect of “you’re so useless, I
might as well make my horse consul!” which

00:15:22.220 --> 00:15:24.140
later writers took literally.

00:15:24.140 --> 00:15:28.209
On the other hand, it’s quite possible that
the tale of Bay of Bauli is true.

00:15:28.209 --> 00:15:32.320
If you’ve not heard it, it’s claimed Caligula
discovered an old prophesy by an astrologer

00:15:32.320 --> 00:15:36.120
that said Caligula had:
“no more chance of becoming emperor than

00:15:36.120 --> 00:15:38.870
of riding a horse across the Bay of Bauli.”

00:15:38.870 --> 00:15:43.570
Once he became emperor, Caligula requisitioned
boats from across Italy, moored them together,

00:15:43.570 --> 00:15:47.660
and turned them into a two or three mile bridge
across the bay.

00:15:47.660 --> 00:15:51.790
He then spent two whole days riding up and
down along it in triumph.

00:15:51.790 --> 00:15:56.610
So many boats were put out of action that
Rome suffered a grain shortage.

00:15:56.610 --> 00:16:01.040
Fun as they are, though, tales like these
often conceal Caligula’s truly dark nature.

00:16:01.040 --> 00:16:02.639
In AD 39, the treasury was empty.

00:16:02.639 --> 00:16:05.690
Too much money had been spent funding Caligula’s
lavish lifestyle.

00:16:05.690 --> 00:16:07.490
So Caligula pulled a fast one.

00:16:07.490 --> 00:16:09.589
He reinstated Tiberius’s treason trials.

00:16:09.589 --> 00:16:12.410
And now they would be even crueler than before.

00:16:12.410 --> 00:16:16.430
People were selected from Roman society at
random and had all their property confiscated

00:16:16.430 --> 00:16:17.430
with no warning.

00:16:17.430 --> 00:16:19.889
The lucky ones were sent into exile.

00:16:19.889 --> 00:16:22.720
The unlucky ones were executed.

00:16:22.720 --> 00:16:25.970
Executions under Caligula were worse than
under any prior emperor.

00:16:25.970 --> 00:16:29.790
Executioners were instructed to keep people
alive as long as possible, slowly torturing

00:16:29.790 --> 00:16:31.699
them to death over several days.

00:16:31.699 --> 00:16:33.890
Relatives were forced to watch these displays.

00:16:33.890 --> 00:16:37.800
In some cases, parents were summoned to see
their children murdered, unable to do anything

00:16:37.800 --> 00:16:42.100
but smile and cheer lest the mad emperor have
them killed too.

00:16:42.100 --> 00:16:46.390
Just as he had on Capri, it seemed Caligula
still loved to see others suffer.

00:16:46.390 --> 00:16:50.230
Yet, gory as this was, it wasn’t quite so
bad as you might believe.

00:16:50.230 --> 00:16:55.019
While Tiberius arranged to have rivals assassinated
as far away as Syria, Caligula basically forgot

00:16:55.019 --> 00:16:57.190
about anyone he wasn’t face to face with.

00:16:57.190 --> 00:17:00.750
His treason trials may have left Rome’s
streets flowing with blood, but the rest of

00:17:00.750 --> 00:17:02.579
the empire continued to function as normal.

00:17:02.579 --> 00:17:08.720
It was in AD 39, at the height of his terror,
that Caligula finally met the woman of his

00:17:08.720 --> 00:17:09.720
dreams.

00:17:09.720 --> 00:17:13.630
Milonia Caesonia was a woman as amoral and
lacking in empathy as her husband.

00:17:13.630 --> 00:17:17.670
Older than Caligula and dedicated to nothing
more than self-indulgence, she managed to

00:17:17.670 --> 00:17:19.510
win the young despot’s heart.

00:17:19.510 --> 00:17:24.010
Although few records of Milonia survive, we
do know that she bore Caligula a daughter

00:17:24.010 --> 00:17:27.730
that the emperor named Julia Drusilla, after
his dead sister.

00:17:27.730 --> 00:17:32.460
We also know that Caligula’s messed up way
of showing affection for his wife was to teasingly

00:17:32.460 --> 00:17:36.580
remind her that he could have her tortured
and killed whenever he wanted to.

00:17:36.580 --> 00:17:40.810
Overall, then, Caligula reached the second
anniversary of his illness in a good place,

00:17:40.810 --> 00:17:44.920
which is another way of saying everyone else
reached it in a terrible place where they

00:17:44.920 --> 00:17:47.180
feared for their lives.

00:17:47.180 --> 00:17:51.200
As October rolled around, the emperor felt
secure enough to turn his back on Rome and

00:17:51.200 --> 00:17:53.510
head to the frontier to do some conquering.

00:17:53.510 --> 00:17:58.010
But Caligula had misjudged just how cowed
by him his opponents were.

00:17:58.010 --> 00:18:01.910
With the madman out the picture, his victims
in the Senate and the Praetorian Guard began

00:18:01.910 --> 00:18:04.390
to whisper to one another about finding a
new emperor.

00:18:04.390 --> 00:18:09.070
Caligula didn’t know it, but his short reign
was already nearly over.

00:18:09.070 --> 00:18:15.970
Death to Tyrants
It’s interesting to wonder how Caligula

00:18:15.970 --> 00:18:19.480
must have felt, returning to the frontier
where he’d been raised, no longer a toy

00:18:19.480 --> 00:18:21.400
soldier, but a conquering emperor.

00:18:21.400 --> 00:18:23.460
At least, that’s one way of looking at it.

00:18:23.460 --> 00:18:27.060
By some accounts, Caligula spent his time
along the Rhine putting down a German revolt

00:18:27.060 --> 00:18:28.520
and invading Gaul.

00:18:28.520 --> 00:18:31.830
On the other hand, different accounts make
it sound like the toy soldier hadn’t grown

00:18:31.830 --> 00:18:32.830
up at all.

00:18:32.830 --> 00:18:37.080
One version has it that Caligula paid soldiers
to dress up like Germans and stage an attack

00:18:37.080 --> 00:18:39.600
so he could “defeat” them and appear a
hero.

00:18:39.600 --> 00:18:43.030
When actual Germans attacked shortly after,
the emperor ran away.

00:18:43.030 --> 00:18:48.130
What’s not in dispute is that, by AD 40,
Caligula had decided to invade Britain.

00:18:48.130 --> 00:18:53.000
At that time, Britannia was still a strange,
damp island not yet under the Roman heel.

00:18:53.000 --> 00:18:56.010
So conquering it would boost Caligula’s
prestige.

00:18:56.010 --> 00:19:01.160
Accordingly, Caligula rode north with 200,000
men, up to the cliffs of Gaul facing the English

00:19:01.160 --> 00:19:02.160
Channel.

00:19:02.160 --> 00:19:05.781
Once there, he told his men to prepare for
a great victory… and then had them all go

00:19:05.781 --> 00:19:08.650
down to the beaches and collect seashells.

00:19:08.650 --> 00:19:12.620
Declaring these were spoils of a great victory
over the sea, Caligula turned around and headed

00:19:12.620 --> 00:19:13.840
back to Rome.

00:19:13.840 --> 00:19:17.180
Back in the Eternal City, the conspiracy against
the emperor was growing.

00:19:17.180 --> 00:19:21.960
It was fed, in part, by Caligula’s belief
that he was literally a God.

00:19:21.960 --> 00:19:26.180
He claimed to communicate with Jupiter, and
ordered statues of himself be placed in every

00:19:26.180 --> 00:19:27.670
temple of the empire.

00:19:27.670 --> 00:19:30.880
When the news reached Judea, it very nearly
caused an armed uprising.

00:19:30.880 --> 00:19:35.350
It was only thanks to Herod Agrippa’s diplomatic
skills that Caligula waived the rule for Judean

00:19:35.350 --> 00:19:37.630
temples and averted civil war.

00:19:37.630 --> 00:19:42.930
But this last flash of sanity was not enough
to save an emperor already so far gone.

00:19:42.930 --> 00:19:47.960
On January 24, 41 AD, the anti-Caligula faction
made their move.

00:19:47.960 --> 00:19:52.030
Outside the Palatine Games, a unit led by
Cassius Chaerea waited until Caligula was

00:19:52.030 --> 00:19:55.410
coming out, then surrounded and stabbed him
to death.

00:19:55.410 --> 00:19:59.390
Before the alarm could be raised, they marched
straight to the house where Milonia and the

00:19:59.390 --> 00:20:02.100
emperor’s baby daughter lived and killed
them both.

00:20:02.100 --> 00:20:06.380
For a moment on that febrile day, it really
looked like Rome’s entire system of emperors

00:20:06.380 --> 00:20:11.020
was about to collapse, that the Senate might
seize the day and restore the Republic.

00:20:11.020 --> 00:20:12.020
But no.

00:20:12.020 --> 00:20:16.310
Cassius Chaerea and his men instead tracked
down Caligula’s uncle, the doddering old

00:20:16.310 --> 00:20:18.690
Claudius, and had him declared emperor.

00:20:18.690 --> 00:20:22.620
The Guard may have been ready to kill Caligula,
but they weren’t ready to kill the system

00:20:22.620 --> 00:20:23.690
that had created him.

00:20:23.690 --> 00:20:30.140
Their failure of nerve would result in another
Caligula 13 years down the line: Emperor Nero.

00:20:30.140 --> 00:20:35.000
That winter day in 41 AD, though, all the
carnage Nero would unleash was still a long

00:20:35.000 --> 00:20:36.000
way away.

00:20:36.000 --> 00:20:39.810
Instead, the Senate focused on purging the
virus of Caligula from the empire.

00:20:39.810 --> 00:20:41.390
Statues of the now-dead emperor were smashed.

00:20:41.390 --> 00:20:43.110
His name was scrubbed from monuments.

00:20:43.110 --> 00:20:47.830
One, likely fictitious, telling has it that
the army even tracked down and killed Caligula’s

00:20:47.830 --> 00:20:48.830
horse.

00:20:48.830 --> 00:20:52.670
Before Claudius had established himself on
the throne, Caligula was gone, almost wiped

00:20:52.670 --> 00:20:53.870
from Roman history.

00:20:53.870 --> 00:20:57.560
The people wanted no more reminders of their
mad tyrant.

00:20:57.560 --> 00:20:59.760
So why do we remember him today?

00:20:59.760 --> 00:21:04.520
Why is Caligula one of the few Roman emperors
nearly everyone can name?

00:21:04.520 --> 00:21:06.790
Part of it is doubtless entertainment value.

00:21:06.790 --> 00:21:10.920
Even if the story about making his horse a
consul is untrue, it’s still amusing.

00:21:10.920 --> 00:21:15.190
But part of it may also be that Caligula serves
as a warning from history.

00:21:15.190 --> 00:21:18.000
Before he took the throne, Caligula was a
quiet nobody.

00:21:18.000 --> 00:21:22.290
When he was made emperor, all he seemingly
wanted was to please people.

00:21:22.290 --> 00:21:25.740
The old phrase has it that absolute power
corrupts absolutely.

00:21:25.740 --> 00:21:28.680
In the life of Caligula, we can see this is
true.

00:21:28.680 --> 00:21:32.640
We may like to believe that a good man could
rule us all justly and wisely, but Caligula’s

00:21:32.640 --> 00:21:34.560
biography suggests otherwise.

00:21:34.560 --> 00:21:38.070
It suggests that almost anyone could become
a monster.

00:21:38.070 --> 00:21:39.880
Perhaps even you or I.

00:21:39.880 --> 00:21:43.570
In the end, Caligula may have only ruled Rome
for a mere four years.

00:21:43.570 --> 00:22:06.930
But his deranged actions during that time
have ensured his name will live on in infamy.

